Bob is technically right in a pedantic sense, but his reasoning is misleading.

At -10°C, water is already frozen (assuming standard pressure). So if the question is "what happens to water at -10°C?" the answer is: it is ice / it stays frozen. It does not "freeze" at -10°C — it is already frozen.

Alice is right that water freezes at 0°C. Bob is right only in the trivial sense that water at -10°C is frozen, but his statement "I'm still right" is confusing the freezing point with the state at -10°C.

So: **Alice is right about the freezing point; Bob is right that water at -10°C is frozen, but wrong to imply that's when it freezes.** [end of text]


